  "notes": "Test data generation approach:\n\n1. **Physical Realism**: All temperatures are in Kelvin (>200K for typical atmospheric conditions, >250K for extreme cases). Pressures decrease with height following approximate hydrostatic balance. Water mixing ratios are constrained to [0, 1] kg/kg with physically realistic values.\n\n2. **Grid Structure**: Grid object (gr) contains dzm (vertical spacing at momentum levels) and zt (heights at thermodynamic levels). The relationship nzt = nzm + 1 is maintained throughout.\n\n3. **Array Dimensions**: All 2D arrays follow shape conventions: (ngrdcol, nzt) for thermodynamic level fields, (ngrdcol, nzm) for momentum level fields. Lscale_max can be scalar or (ngrdcol,) array.\n\n4. **Nominal Cases**: Cover typical boundary layer conditions with moderate TKE (0.3-1.5 m\u00b2/s\u00b2), realistic temperature profiles (280-310K), and moisture decreasing with height.\n\n5. **Edge Cases**: Include zero TKE (testing minimum length scale behavior), very dry conditions (near-zero moisture), high moisture (approaching saturation), and minimum vertical resolution.\n\n6. **Special Cases**: High altitude/low pressure conditions, strong temperature inversions (testing stability effects), large grids (computational efficiency), and extreme TKE gradients.\n\n7. **Parameter Ranges**: mu (turbulence parameter) varies 0.4-0.7, lmin (minimum length) 0.5-2.0m, saturation_formula alternates between 1 and 2, l_implemented is always true for these tests.\n\n8. **Vertical Profiles**: Temperature generally increases with height (stable stratification), moisture decreases with height, pressure decreases hydrostatically, TKE typically peaks in mid-levels or decreases with height.\n\n9. **Multi-column Tests**: Include varied conditions across columns to test spatial heterogeneity handling.\n\n10. **Constants**: ZLMIN (0.1m) represents the absolute minimum mixing length scale that outputs should respect."
}